.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-volume 1  "tapectl-volume " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume \- Manage volumes and tape operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Manage volumes and tape operations
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-init(1)
Initialize a new volume (write ID thunk to tape)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-write(1)
Write staged data to volume
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-resume(1)
Resume an interrupted write session
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-abort(1)
Abandon a volume\*(Aqs unfinished write session for good
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-verify(1)
Verify volume contents via the keyless chain walk (seal \-> front index \-> content)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-identify(1)
Identify the loaded tape: print its File 0 and what this catalog makes of it
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-move(1)
Move a volume to a location
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-retire(1)
Retire a volume (with impact analysis)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-read\-slices(1)
Read encrypted slices from a volume into staging (then use `volume write` to write them)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-compact\-read(1)
Read live encrypted slices from a volume to staging (compaction step 1)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-compact\-write(1)
Write compaction slices from staging to destination (compaction step 2)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-plan(1)
Show bin\-packing plan for pending staged data
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-compact\-finish(1)
Retire source volume after compaction (compaction step 3)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-compact(1)
Interactive compaction: read + write + finish in one flow
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-rewrite(1)
Compact a volume IN PLACE, on its own cartridge (stage, retire, erase, write back)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-deposit(1)
Record and inspect WAREHOUSE DEPOSITS of sealed volumes (ADR\-0006)
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-list(1)
List every volume, most recently written first
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-info(1)
The dossier for one volume: capacity, cartridge, place, units, writes, verifies
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-import(1)
Record a pre\-existing volume in the catalog
.TP
tapectl\-volume\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
